Max distance between same elements
Given an array arr[], the task is to find the maximum distance between two occurrences of an element. 
If no element has two occurrences, then return 0.

Logic

We create a HashMap<element, firstIndex>.
If the element is seen for the first time, store its index.
If the element is seen again:
Do not update the HashMap.

Calculate the distance using:
currentIndex - firstIndex
Update the maximum distance if needed.

The important point is:
Never replace the first occurrence in the HashMap.

Why?
Because we want
last occurrence - first occurrence
If you overwrite the first index, you'll lose the maximum possible distance.

Code:

class Solution {
    public int maxDistance(int[] arr) {
        // Code here
        HashMap<Integer,Integer> map = new HashMap<>();
        int maxDistance=0;
        for(int i=0;i<arr.length;i++){
            int num=arr[i];
            if(!map.containsKey(num)){
                map.put(num,i);
            }
            else{
                maxDistance = Math.max(maxDistance, Math.abs(i-map.get(num)));
            } 
        }
        
        return maxDistance;
    }
}
